From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 02:01:49 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/14] feat(web): H.264 in the graphics pipeline, decoded by
 WebCodecs

The web client's graphics pipeline advertised CAPVERSION_8 only, so servers
sent RemoteFX Progressive, ClearCodec and planar, all decoded in
WebAssembly on the main thread. With the new `h264Decoder(decoder)`
extension it also advertises the H.264 versions, V10.7 down to V10 and
V8.1 with AVC420, and a `WebCodecsH264Decoder` decodes the H.264 with the
browser's VideoDecoder (hardware-accelerated where the platform has it).
Windows only streams H.264 to V10 and later: to V8.1 it confirms the set
without AVC420.

ironrdp-egfx: `GraphicsPipelineClient::with_external_h264` hands AVC
frames out instead of decoding them in `process` (`take_h264_frames`), for
decoders that are asynchronous or live elsewhere. The caller returns each
frame's region pixels with `complete_h264_frame`. Commands after a frame,
its EndFrame and so its FrameAcknowledge wait until then, since surface
commands read the pixels the frame produces and the acknowledgement paces
the server; frames can be decoded ahead, and are applied in order. The
queue depth in acknowledgements counts the frames still waiting.

AVC444 (which V10 and later imply) is handed out as its YUV420 main view:
both views are one H.264 stream, so every access unit goes to the decoder,
but only the main view's picture is shown. Combining the auxiliary view
into 4:4:4 is left out, so AVC444 comes out with 4:2:0 chroma.

A new `h264` module prepares the bitstream for platform decoders:
MS-RDPEGFX specifies Annex B (a length-prefixed chain is converted), and an
SPS without a VUI bitstream_restriction gets one declaring
max_num_reorder_frames = 0, as WebRTC does. Without it, conformant decoders
(Chromium's, in front of VideoToolbox, VA-API and D3D11) hold every picture
until the decoded picture buffer is full: with a VUI-less High profile
stream, no frame came out of Chrome's VideoDecoder before the next went in.

ironrdp-session: `ActiveStage::take_graphics_h264_frames` and
`complete_graphics_h264_frame`, which sends the acknowledgements and drains
the compositor as `process` does.

ironrdp-web and iron-remote-desktop-rdp: the session loop hands frames to
the decoder object as they arrive and completes them as it calls back.
`WebCodecsH264Decoder` keeps a VideoDecoder per surface, starts each at a
key frame, reads the regions back as RGBA (`VideoFrame.copyTo`, or a canvas
where the browser can't convert), and decodes as full-range BT.709
whatever the stream signals (MS-RDPEGFX 3.3.8.3.1). A decoder error, or a
frame that doesn't come out within `stallMs`, fails it, which ends the
session with an error: the application can reconnect without H.264.
`h264Supported()` asks VideoDecoder.isConfigSupported first.
